      "keyInsight": "We are walking survival engines built for scarcity, suddenly dropped into an endless all-you-can-eat buffet. For thousands of years, dopamine had one clear mission: keep you hunting, gathering, and moving toward scarce treats. It was nature's push notification, whispering that another berry bush might save your life tomorrow. Today, that ancient engine has backfired. You do not have to brave winter frosts or track deer through muddy forests anymore. Instead, endless dopamine flows right through shiny screens, salty snacks, and one-click deliveries without moving a single muscle. The hidden catch is wild. Your brain treats every buzzing alert like fresh honey discovered in the wild. We run on high-octane rocket fuel while sitting parked in traffic. The relentless urge to pursue more never shuts off because our biology still thinks the pantry is empty. In a world with infinite treats, an uncalibrated appetite quickly turns into a prison.",
      "content": "Imagine driving a vintage sports car designed exclusively for rough mountain trails, then getting stuck on a flat conveyor belt. You hit the gas pedal out of habit, but the engine just screeches in place. That sports car is your nervous system. In ancient forests, finding sugar, warmth, or good tools took hours of hard labor. Dopamine acted like the starter motor, urging your ancestors to keep walking over the next hill to survive.\n\nNow look around your living room. The wilderness has vanished, replaced by a 24-hour convenience store that never sleeps. You can order warm pizza, stream entire television seasons, and chat with strangers without leaving your sofa. Every bright package and chime taps the exact same reward button that ancient berries did. Our world turned into a modern sugar factory, but our internal machinery never received the software upgrade.\n\nBecause the chase has no natural finish line anymore, our reward circuits stay locked in overdrive. When good things were rare, wanting more kept you alive. When good things are endless, wanting more wears out the gearbox. We have become restless runners on an electric treadmill, sweating profusely while going nowhere. Recognizing this mismatch is the very first step toward catching your breath.",
