      "content": "Think of pain tolerance like baking bread in a warm oven. If you pull the loaf out whenever the heat rises, you end up with raw, sticky dough that collapses. Discomfort is simply the heat that bakes resilience into character. Yet our modern culture sells endless ways to numb out the moment things feel even slightly heavy or boring. Waiting in an elevator for twenty seconds now feels like a crisis without a screen in hand.\n\nWhenever quiet moments appear, our natural impulse is to apply a digital bandage. We pop in wireless earbuds while washing dishes, scroll through shopping sites when a conversation lags, and binge shows when loneliness whispers. We run from our feelings like hikers sprinting away from thunderclouds. But feelings are not predators. They are just passing weather patterns that blow through when left alone.\n\nBy dodging every uncomfortable second, we pay an invisible tax. The mental muscles required to endure boredom or grief wither away from lack of use. Eventually, the tiniest annoyance feels like a flat tire on a rainy highway. When you stop running away from small aches, you discover something remarkable: discomfort shrinks the moment you turn around and greet it.",
